{
  "image_name": "2ec2908fde56d7a99d0de9d2a7511f13.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how major global-change drivers alter forest soils and microbial communities, and shows that their importance differs among boreal, temperate, and tropical forests. Across the top, six drivers are highlighted: rising CO₂, warming, fires, drought, nitrogen deposition, and pest outbreaks. The text indicates that elevated CO₂ can increase plant production and carbon inputs belowground, but also speeds microbial activity and nitrogen cycling. Warming tends to reduce soil microbial biomass, stimulate decomposition of soil organic matter, and shift microbial community composition. Fires remove carbon and lower nutrient availability, drought reduces productivity and microbial biomass while changing community structure, nitrogen deposition can boost productivity but suppress decomposition and alter fungal–bacterial balance, and pest outbreaks are linked to climate-stressed trees and more pathogens.\n\nThe lower half compares forest types with icons marking the main stressors for each biome. Boreal forests are associated especially with CO₂ increase, warming, fire, and nitrogen deposition. Temperate forests show a broader mix including drought and pests. Tropical forests are mainly linked with CO₂ increase, warming, drought, and fire. Illustrated trees and soil organisms emphasize that aboveground climate pressures are tightly connected to belowground microbes and carbon cycling.",
